## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's expertise in testing complex streaming systems with stringent privacy and availability requirements. It probes understanding of end-to-end data pipeline validation, privacy compliance, and operational resilience.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency and availability in distributed streaming systems
- Privacy controls including PII masking and data retention policies
- Data drift detection and disaster recovery strategies
- Synthetic data generation and test automation patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unit test for PII masking logic
describe('PIIMasking', () => {
  const masking = new PIIMasking();

  test('masks email addresses completely', () => {
    const input = { user_id: '123', email: 'john.doe@example.com' };
    const result = masking.mask(input);
    expect(result.email).toBe('j***@***.com');
    expect(result.user_id).toBe('123');
  });

  test('handles nested PII objects', () => {
    const input = { profile: { name: 'Jane', ssn: '123-45-6789' } };
    const result = masking.mask(input);
    expect(result.profile.ssn).toBe('***-**-6789');
  });
});

// Integration test for Kafka-Flink-TimescaleDB flow
describe('PipelineIntegration', () => {
  test('processes events within 200ms latency', async () => {
    const start = Date.now();
    await produceTestEvent({ user_id: '123', action: 'click' });
    await waitForProcessedMetric('123');
    const latency = Date.now() - start;
    expect(latency).toBeLessThan(200);
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical testing strategy, not just theory. Meta wants engineers who can balance speed and confidence in testing.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates mention: unit tests for individual components/utils, integration tests for component interactions, E2E for user workflows. They should discuss coverage trade-offs and mutation testing for quality assurance.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Unit test example
describe('Button', () => {
  it('renders with correct text', () => {
    render(<Button>Click me</Button>)
    expect(screen.getByRole('button')).toHaveTextContent('Click me')
  })
})

// Integration test example
describe('Form submission', () => {
  it('submits form with valid data', async () => {
    const { getByRole, getByLabelText } = render(<ContactForm />)
    fireEvent.change(getByLabelText('Email'), { target: { value: 'test@example.com' } })
    fireEvent.click(getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }))
    await waitFor(() => expect(mockSubmit).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ email: 'test@example.com' }))
  })
})
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle testing third-party integrations?
- When would you use mutation testing vs traditional coverage?
- How do you determine which E2E tests are worth maintaining?

